Repository information and Git
Git is a command line program that allows you to track versions of any code or plain text document that you create. 
It tracks who made which particular changes, the time and date of the modifications, how they differ, where the changes were made, the directory/repository and the specific file. Helpful when wanting to revert or view a previous version of a project. Restores deleted files, can find new bugs in programs. GitHub is a website that provides remote git repositories. You can create your own public remote repositories for free through git hub. allows for easy syncing and collaboration on projects. 
